<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="
          http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
          https://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.23.xsd">

    <!--
      "Ime za igru" — the name a player wears at the online card table,
      separate from the account display name.

      Why its own table and not a column on user_profiles:

        * HALF THE PLAYERS HAVE NO PROFILE ROW AND NEVER WILL. The game server
          seats guests — people who never signed in — under an id it derives
          from a secret their browser keeps: `guest:<64 hex>`. A guest has no
          Firebase account, so `user_profiles` (whose PK *is* the Firebase UID)
          has nowhere to put their name. Keying on the game server's own uid
          string covers both kinds of player with one row shape.

        * The once-a-week rule has to key on the same string. For a guest that
          browser secret is the only stable identity there is, so the limit and
          the name must live on the same key or the limit is trivially reset.

      `game_uid` is varchar(128): the width `game_result_players.uid` already
      uses for the same identifiers (§8.3 of game/README.md). A Firebase UID is
      28 characters and `guest:` + 64 hex is 70, so 128 is headroom, not a
      guess — and it is a hard cap on what an internal caller can make us store.

      `name` is varchar(16) because the protocol's LIMITS.playerNameMax is 16.
      The service rejects anything longer rather than truncating; the column
      width is the second line of defence, not the first.

      Deliberately NOT unique. Two players may both call themselves "Ivan" —
      this is a label above a seat, not a handle anyone is addressed by, and a
      uniqueness constraint would turn a cosmetic choice into a land grab.

      No index beyond the primary key: every read and write of this table is a
      lookup by `game_uid`.

      tableExists / MARK_RAN, like the other tables here, so a database where
      this was created by hand is a no-op rather than a startup failure.
    -->

    <changeSet id="2026-09-09-game-names" author="mrodek">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not>
                <tableExists tableName="game_names"/>
            </not>
        </preConditions>

        <createTable tableName="game_names">
            <!-- The game server's id for the player: a Firebase UID for a
                 signed-in user, `guest:<64 hex>` for a guest. -->
            <column name="game_uid" type="varchar(128)">
                <constraints primaryKey="true" nullable="false"
                             primaryKeyName="pk_game_names"/>
            </column>

            <column name="name" type="varchar(16)">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>

            <!-- When the name was last set. The once-per-7-days rule is
                 measured from here, so it is NOT a plain @UpdateTimestamp:
                 a write that changes nothing else must still move it, and a
                 write refused by the rule must not. -->
            <column name="changed_at" type="timestamptz">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
        </createTable>

        <rollback>
            <dropTable tableName="game_names"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
